{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n6le03ob02re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n6le03ob02re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob02re2img01.jpg", "text": "<strong>Marie</strong>: If I had paid more attention in class, I would have understood Javascript syntax. Kyle, could you help me with this?<br/>\n<strong>Kyle</strong>: Sure. Syntax is the group of rules needed for sentence making. In the case of programming languages, syntax isn't very flexible and demands statements to be written painstakingly.<br/>\n<strong>Marie</strong>: So, if I follow the rules of syntax, working will be easier for me.<br/>\n<strong>Kyle</strong>: Right! When using JavaScript, statements are placed between \u2018<  script  >\u2026<  /script  >'. These tags can be written anywhere in the web page, however, it's recommendable to keep them within the <  head  > tags. If the browser program reads the <  script  > tags, it'll know it has to interpret this text as a script.", "time": "100"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob02re2img02.jpg", "text": "<strong>Marie</strong>: Ok, I think I'd better jot it down. I know that tags have two attributes: the language that specifies the scripting language I'm using such as javascript and XHTML, among others; and the type that specifies the scripting language in use, for instance, text/javascript. Any other thing I need to bear in mind?<br/>\n<strong>Kyle</strong>: Mmmm, if the programming language is case-sensitive, you need to be aware of capital letters. Do you know what that implies?<br/>\n<strong>Marie</strong>: Yes. JavaScript is indeed case-sensitive. Identifiers like language keywords and function names must be written in capital letters in a consistent manner. If the same word is written twice differently, the code will read them as two different elements. So I need to be careful when writing lower and upper case letters. And how we use punctuation is pivotal for the code to work well, isn't it?", "time": "61000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob02re2img03.jpg", "text": "<strong>Kyle</strong>: Yes. Statements are separated by semicolons. If they are in different lines, a semicolon is not needed. That's something you really need to be mindful of. On the other hand, JavaScript doesn't read whitespaces and line breaks, therefore, if you write them, the result won't be any different. <br/>\n<strong>Marie</strong>: Typing spaces is where people make most mistakes too. I think I got everything. If I need your help with JavaScript, I'll ask you again. Thank you so much for helping me. <br/>\n<strong>Kyle</strong>: You're welcome.", "time": "129000"}]}, "type": "slideshow"}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "type": "WLC", "questions": [], "stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "img_path": "", "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": "I can use script tags for JavaScript.  ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about important attributes for script tags in JavaScript.  ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about case sensitivity in JavaScript.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "Why do you think it is important to be very careful when writing code in JavaScript? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "What rules for writing code do you know for JavaScript?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct"}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask your partne